    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-h/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-h/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"In a certain Crown Court 95% of the defendants being tried have actually committed the crime they are being tried for. For those who committed the crime the probability of being found guilty is 90% and for those who did not commit the crime the probability of being found guilty is 5%. ( ) a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen defendant will be found guilty. 3 b) Given that a randomly chosen defendant was found guilty, find the probability that the defendant committed the crime. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:570.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-h/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Drawing a net diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:508.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-h/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Let C mean the defendant committed the crime and G mean a guilty verdict: (a) Use both routes to a guilty verdict: P(G) = 0.95(0.90) + 0.05(0.05) = 0.855 + 0.0025 [M1] [M1] [A1] = 0.8575 . (b) Restrict the denominator to all guilty verdicts: Of these, the branch for an actual crime has probability P(C ∩G) = 0.95(0.90) = 0.855. P(C | G) = 0.855/0.8575 ≈0.9971 . [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "In a certain Crown Court 95% of the defendants being tried have actually committed the crime they are being tried for. For those who committed the crime the probability of being found guilty is 90% and for those who did not commit the crime the probability of being found guilty is 5%. ( ) a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen defendant will be found guilty. 3 b) Given that a randomly chosen defendant was found guilty, find the probability that the defendant committed the crime. ( ) 3"
  },
