      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-h/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Use class midpoints 4, 6.5, 8, 10, 14.5, giving P f = 64, P fx = 508, and P fx2 = 4561. (a) The mean and standard deviation estimates are s 2 ¯x = 508 4561 508 64 ≈7.94 miles , σ = − ≈2.87 miles . 64 64 [M1] Use the weighted first moment [A1] calculate the estimated mean [M1] use the weighted second moment and subtract the squared mean [M1] take the square root (b) The median is at cumulative frequency 32. [M1] Interpolate in the median class using (m −7.5)/(8.5 −7.5) = (32 −26)/(45 −26). There are 26 journeys below 7.5 miles and 19 in 7.5–8.5, so 7.5 + (32 −26)/19 ≈7.82 miles . [M1] [A1] (c) The original class has width 3 and density 12/3 = 4. [B1] The last has width 6 and density 6/6 = 1.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-h/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Its base is 1.2(6/3) = 2.4 cm [A1] and its height is 5(1/4) = 1.25 cm . [A1] (d) The IQR is 9.19 −6.07 = 3.12. The lower fence is 6.07 −1.5(3.12) = 1.39 [B1] and the upper fence is 9.19 + 1.5(3.12) = 13.87. [A1] There are no lower outliers: The top class straddles the upper fence, so upper outliers are possible and the grouped data cannot confirm them: [A1] (e) The estimated mean of 7.94 is slightly above the median of 7.82, suggesting slight positive skew: [B1] [A1] A longer right tail would be a concern for a symmetric normal model, though the averages alone provide weak evidence: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 16 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The distances rounded to the nearest mile, of 64 journeys covered by a taxi driver during a given week, is summarized in the table below. Distance Frequency (nearest mile) 3 – 5 12 6 – 7 14 8 19 9 – 11 13 12 – 17 6 a) Estimate the mean and the standard deviation of these weekly distances. ( ) 4 No credit will be given unless appropriate formulae are used in this part. b) Estimate, by linear interpolation, the median value. ( ) 3 In a histogram drawn for the above data, the class 3 – 5 is represented by a rectangle of base length 1.2 cm and height 5 cm . c) Find the base length and height of the rectangle representing the class 12 – 17 in the same histogram. ( ) 3 It is further given that the lower and upper quartiles of these distances are 6.07 and 9.19, respectively. ( ) d) Investigate the possibility of any outliers. 3 e) By considering the skewness using the averages, discuss briefly whether the above set of data can be modelled by a normal distribution. ( ) 3 "
